allium-inspect calls allium's own parser and analyser as a library rather than
reimplementing them, so the binaries built from this repository contain that
code. The MIT License requires its copyright notice to travel with it, which is
what this file is for: ship it alongside any binary you distribute.

The `allium` command-line tool is a separate program. allium-inspect runs it for
two of its commands and does not include it.


The rest of the Rust dependency tree comes from crates.io under the terms
`cargo deny check licenses` enforces; run `just deny` for the current list.

The binary also embeds the built browser interface, which is npm code that no
Rust tool sees. Its licences are listed in full in `ui/public/THIRD-PARTY.txt`,
generated by `just third-party` and served by the running tool at
`/THIRD-PARTY.txt`. Two things there are worth naming here:

  elkjs — https://github.com/kieler/elkjs
  Offered under `EPL-2.0 OR GPL-3.0-or-later`. This project takes it under the
  Eclipse Public License 2.0. Section 3.2 of that licence requires recipients of
  the binary to be told the licence and to be able to obtain the source; the
  notice file and the link above are how that is done.

  d3-* (ISC), d3-ease (BSD-3-Clause), aria-query and axobject-query
  (Apache-2.0). All require their copyright notice to be reproduced in a binary
  redistribution, and the bundler strips every legal comment, so the generated
  notice file is the only place those notices survive.

So: distributing a binary built from this repository means shipping this file
and `THIRD-PARTY.txt` with it.
